<p>Thank you.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>My concern with your idea for test logs is that it feels…kinda like they aren't adding much</p>
</blockquote>
<p>True, I felt like I had to pitch in logs, since you seemed to want a more story-telling article, but they actually won't add much.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I'm not sure narrowing the list of foods down from 'all foods' to 'some foods' is interesting reading</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I agree, this observation doesn't deserve a log. I was thinking more of referring to such test in the SCP effect's description, alluding to the discomfort and possible mouth injuries that the SCP causes.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>it feels like you're wanting to keep this short, which'll likely make evocative tooth writing hard</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't feel like that would be an issue. But it does not have to be an excessively short article to the point I can't convey the ideas.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I'm a little unclear also on why the coins are radioactive?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It's only a small detail. It would be hypothetized in the article that this is linked to the transmutation, which happens at the atomic stage.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>it's kinda missing that bit right at the end where a reader goes from 'ew that's weird' to 'oh god what the fuck', y'know? It feels like it needs just, a final way to extrapolate this from 'creates teeth in your food that you can exchange for money' into a horrifying conclusion?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I see what you mean. I didn't think all SCPs required a horryfing bit. I can think of something :<br />
how about subjects exposed to this SCP start developping an unhealthy fascination for teeth, ranging from inspecting their smile in the mirror several times per day and increased dental hygiene at a minimal exposure, to wanting to tear off their own teeth to place them in the SCP at the most extreme exposure.<br />
Subjects strongly influenced by the SCP would want to collect as many coins as possible from the SCP.</p>
<p>What do you think of this?</p>

